Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
  "Week 1   Projective Geometry",
  StyleBox["\nComplex numbers",
    FontSize->18]
}], "Title",
  Evaluatable->False,
  PageBreakAbove->True,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "\n",
  StyleBox["\[Copyright] Dana Scott 1993/1997/1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\n\nNotebook  last edited: ",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["25 August 1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Subsubtitle"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Basic Definitions and Properties", "Section",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["What is a Complex Number?", "Subsection",
  PageBreakAbove->True,
  PageBreakBelow->Automatic],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The imaginary unit", "Subsubsection",
  PageBreakBelow->Automatic],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We begin a review of some basics, which we hope are already familiar.  \
What may be new, however, is how to carry out these operation in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "An arbitrary complex number ",
  StyleBox["z == x + I y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " consists of two parts:\n\tthe ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " value, which is called the ",
  StyleBox["real part",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and \n\tthe ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " value which is called the ",
  StyleBox["imaginary part",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".\nThe quantities ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are arbitrary real numbers.  Two complex numbers are ",
  StyleBox["equal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if, and only if, their real and imaginary parts are respectively equal. "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the ",
  StyleBox["unit",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the imaginary part is labeled by ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  It is a complex number in itself, and the term ",
  StyleBox["I y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " definitely means \"",
  StyleBox["I times y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\" and ",
  StyleBox["x + I y ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "also",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "definitely",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "means \"",
  StyleBox["x plus I y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\".  ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We will have to ",
  StyleBox["justify",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " this use of the algebraic notation below.  In ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the real and imaginary parts are denoted by ",
  StyleBox["Re[z] ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and ",
  StyleBox["Im[z]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", respectively.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " 3.0 is also able to output ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (and other mathematical symbols) in a new font to make formulas look more \
like standard mathematical notation.  In the example below select the cell \
and do ",
  StyleBox["Command-T",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to see the \"traditional\" notation.  We are not using it, as having too \
many fonts confuses the way users type into notebooks.  Doing Command-N \
returns the cell to \"standard\" notation."
}], "SmallText",
  TextAlignment->Left,
  TextJustification->0,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[" Here is a first example:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(z = \(-12\) - 17\ I\), 
    \(Re[z]\), 
    \(Im[z]\), 
    \(z == Re[z] + I\ Im[z]\), 
    \(z =. \)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Complex numbers arise in many mathematical and physical situations.   A \
simple mathematical example is the problem of solving the quadratic equation \
like",
  StyleBox[" z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  StyleBox[" + 1 == 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".   Obviously,  ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  StyleBox[" == -1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and this equation has no ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " solutions.  However, if we introduce an \"imaginary\" number ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " such that ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  StyleBox[" == -1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then the two solutions to our quadratic equation are ",
  StyleBox["z == I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["z == -I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  With the rule ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  StyleBox[" == -1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " it is easily verified that these solutions do satisfy the equation.   \
Let's check this using ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  As expected the roots are ",
  StyleBox["purely imaginary",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(solutions = Solve[z\^2 + 1 == 0, z]\n\), 
    \(z\^2 + 1 /. solutions\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So the solutions do in fact solve the equation and the roots of the \
quadratic equation are found by invoking the subtitutions returned by ",
  StyleBox["Solve",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(z /. solutions\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, if we replace ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " by ",
  StyleBox["z/2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we get again purely imaginary roots."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[\((z\/2)\)\^2 + 1 == 0, z]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But, if we replace ",
  StyleBox["z ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "by ",
  StyleBox["z/2 \[Dash] 5",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we get as a root a combination of real and imaginary parts.  Moreover \
the expanded equation looks quite different from the original one."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Solve[\((z\/2 - 5)\)\^2 + 1 == 0, z]\), 
    \(4 \((\((z\/2 - 5)\)\^2 + 1)\) == 0\  // \ ExpandAll\)}], "Input"],

Cell["The questions we have to review and explain, however, are: ", "Text",
  TextAlignment->Left],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["(a) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Why is the expansion of ",
  StyleBox["arithmetic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["algebra",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " from the real to the complex domain is ",
  StyleBox["consistent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "? and "
}], "Text",
  TextAlignment->Left,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["(b)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " Why it is ",
  StyleBox["useful",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "? "
}], "Text",
  TextAlignment->Left,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Complex arithmetic", "Subsubsection",
  PageBreakBelow->Automatic],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the arithmetic functions (as well as many other functions) have all been \
implemented to deal with complex quantities.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Comment:",
  StyleBox[
  " In the phrase \"arithmetic functions\" be sure to pronounce the adjective \
",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "ar-ith",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "MET",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "ic",
  StyleBox[".  In other words you do ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "a-RITH-me-tic",
  StyleBox[" with ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "ar-ith",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "MET",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "ic",
  StyleBox[" functions.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "SmallText",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell["For example, we have these results needing explanation:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\((2 + I\ 3)\) + \((\(-5\) - I\ 9)\)\n\), 
    \(\((2 + I\ 3)\)\ \((\(-5\) - I\ 9)\)\n\), 
    \(\(2 + I\ 3\)\/\(\(-5\) - I\ 9\)\n\), 
    \(\((2 + I\ 3)\)\^11\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Here is the first definition:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["Addition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["multiplication",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of complex numbers is calculated in general in terms of the ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["imaginary",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " parts as follows:"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ComplexSimplify[\((a + I\ b)\) + \((c + I\ d)\)]\), 
    \(ComplexSimplify[\((a + I\ b)\)*\((c + I\ d)\)]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The command ",
  StyleBox["ComplexSimplify ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "treats all the variables as ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  and, after an expansion, collects real and imaginary parts conveniently \
together .  The command is needed to obtain ",
  StyleBox["symbolic",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " results."
}], "SmallText",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning:",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " 3.0 has a built-in function ",
  StyleBox["ComplexExpand",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", but a bug in the function does not  collect the real and imaginary parts \
together and has some other problems.  The related operator ",
  StyleBox["ComplexSimplify",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " was defined in ",
  StyleBox["Implementations.nb",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to overcome these difficulties (we hope)."
}], "SmallText",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 0, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To explain the definitions above, we remark first that the expression ",
  StyleBox["x + I y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is being treated algebraically as a ",
  StyleBox["linear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["combination",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the two units ",
  StyleBox["1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Thus, in ",
  StyleBox["adding",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " two such numbers the coefficients of  ",
  StyleBox["1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are just added separately.  To see how ",
  StyleBox["multiplying",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is to be done, we can just expand ordinary polynomial expressions of the \
form ",
  StyleBox["x + J y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", where ",
  StyleBox["J",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["non-special",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " quantity."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((Collect[#1, J]&)\)[Expand[\((a + J\ b)\)\ \((c + J\ d)\)]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, if we replace ",
  StyleBox["J",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " by ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we just have to remember that the imaginary unit satisfies ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  StyleBox[" == -1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Then we get the real and imaginary parts as shown before.  Let us also \
try some powers."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(I\^Range[0, 12]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Question:",
  StyleBox["  Why are there only four possible answers?",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["Here are some general powers:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Expand[\((a + J\ b)\)\^6]\), 
    \(ComplexSimplify[\((a + I\ b)\)\^6]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["ComplexSimplify",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " also works for negative exponents, and so we use it to find the \
definitions."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The ",
  StyleBox["reciprocal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a complex number, and the ",
  StyleBox["quotient",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of two complex numbers are defined as follows."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ComplexSimplify[1\/\(a + I\ b\)]\), 
    \(ComplexSimplify[\(a + I\ b\)\/\(c + I\ d\)]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Let us see if these definitions are algebraically reasonable.  \
First, we try the reciprocal.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Together[
      Expand[\((a + b\ J)\)\ 
          \((a\/\(a\^2 + b\^2\) - \(J\ b\)\/\(a\^2 + b\^2\))\)]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Ah, ha!  When ",
  StyleBox["J == I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then the result is indeed ",
  StyleBox["1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  This works, of course, ",
  StyleBox["provided",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the denominator ",
  StyleBox["does not vanish",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".   Since  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{
              SuperscriptBox[
                StyleBox["a",
                  FontWeight->"Bold",
                  FontSlant->"Plain"], "2"], "+", 
              SuperscriptBox[
                StyleBox["b",
                  FontWeight->"Bold",
                  FontSlant->"Plain"], "2"]}], 
            FormBox["==",
              "TraditionalForm"], "0"}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], TraditionalForm]]],
  " if and only if ",
  StyleBox["a == b == 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we see that multiplicative inverses exist for all complex numbers ",
  StyleBox["different from",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["0 == 0 + I 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  And this is what makes ",
  StyleBox["division",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " possible.  In fact, division is just the product of one number times the \
reciprocal of the other."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ComplexSimplify[
      \((a + I\ b)\)\ \((c\/\(c\^2 + d\^2\) - \(I\ d\)\/\(c\^2 + d\^2\))\)]
      \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And sometimes ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " does some ",
  StyleBox["surprising simplifications",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "!"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Together[%]\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Laws of Algebra", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The polynomial representation", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["The Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We have been freely using all the usual ",
  StyleBox["equational laws",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of algebra while working with complex numbers.  The question is:  ",
  StyleBox["What gives us leave to do so?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["The Answer. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["Polynomial algebra",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " justifies the use of familiar principles of algebra",
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->GrayLevel[0.666667]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the quantities ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["i ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " have different types:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head/@{I, i}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Complex",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is something new.  We are asking why it is justified to expand the use of \
all algebraic rules and notions to these new quantities.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For example, if we take ",
  StyleBox["i",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " just as a ",
  StyleBox["symbol",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (",
  StyleBox["i.e., ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "algebraic ",
  StyleBox["variable",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "), then we know what the laws of algebra will do."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[\((a + i\ b)\)\^6]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we know from the study of basic algebra that \[LongDash] just as for \
the numbers \[LongDash] polynomial expressions ",
  StyleBox["do satisfy",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the laws of algebra (oh, such laws as the ",
  StyleBox["Associative ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "and",
  StyleBox[" Distributive Laws",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "), because that is the way polynomial algebra is ",
  StyleBox["defined",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  In other words, any algebraic ",
  StyleBox["identity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that is valid for ",
  StyleBox["numbers",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " remains valid when ",
  StyleBox["polynomials",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are substituted for the variables.  ",
  StyleBox[
  "This last assertion can be made rigorous in another way by regarding \
polynomial ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["experssions",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" as definitions of polynomial ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["functions",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".  The arithmetic of functions is defined ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["argumentwise",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[
  ", and\[LongDash]as a general principle\[LongDash]all the usual laws go \
over to the arithmetic of functions.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now here is the ",
  StyleBox["new",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " idea:  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We say that two (arbitrary) polynomials are ",
  StyleBox["equivalent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["modulo",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a (given) polynomial if they ",
  StyleBox["differ by a",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (polynomial) ",
  StyleBox["multiple",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the given polynomial.  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
The definition of equivalence modulo a polynomial has this \
algebraic formulation:   \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Two polynomials ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox[" g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["equivalent modulo",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "a (fixed) polynomial ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " if, and only if, we have \n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f - g == q\ p\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " for some polynomial multiple of ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  TextAlignment->Left,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The particular multiple to be chosen of course depends on ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  This also means that the polynomial ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["divides",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "evenly into ",
  StyleBox["f - g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".   Now, the test in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for this with polynomials ",
  StyleBox["with integer coefficients",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is very convenient: \n\t",
  StyleBox["PolynomialMod[f - g, p] == 0",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".   \nIn general, we are considering polynomials with arbitrary ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "coefficients here, however."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is the ",
  StyleBox["zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " polynomial, then the relationship ",
  StyleBox["modulo",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["p ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is obviously just ",
  StyleBox["equality",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of polynomials.   If  ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is the ",
  StyleBox["unit",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " polynomial ",
  StyleBox["1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (or any real constant), then the relationship ",
  StyleBox["modulo",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["p ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "holds for ",
  StyleBox["all pairs",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of polynomials.  These two equivalence realtions we regard as ",
  StyleBox["trivial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We will be interested the non-trivial cases.  In books on algebra, this \
lemma is proved."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Lemma.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The product of two non-zero polynomials (in any number of variables) can \
never be ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and the product of  non-constant polynomial with a non-zero polynomial \
can never be constant."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The proof is a somewhat fussy induction on the number of variables in the \
polynomials together with the calculation of the ",
  StyleBox["degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a polynomial (in a given variable).  We will have to accept the truth \
of the lemma as being quite plausible here.  We actually only need to \
consider polynomials in one variable, where the truth of the lemma is clear \
by what we know about the behavior of polynomial functions."
}], "SmallText",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
The following theorems and their proofs may also be familiar from \
algebra courses:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Equivalence modulo a non-zero, non-constant polynomial is a non-trivial \
equivalence realtion."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Proof",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Let the non-zero, non-constant polynomial be ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        The relation is not the ",
  StyleBox["identity relation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", because the definition makes ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " equivalent to ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        The relation is not the ",
  StyleBox["universal relation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", because\[LongDash]by the lemma\[LongDash] ",
  StyleBox["1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " equivalent to ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        That shows the ",
  StyleBox["non-triviality",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the relationship."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
        We next have to check the three properties of being an \
equivalence relation.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        ",
  StyleBox["[Reflexivity]",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["f - f == 0 == 0 p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "       ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  StyleBox["[Symmetry]",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  "  Suppose ",
  StyleBox["f - g == q p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Then ",
  StyleBox["g - f == -(f - g) == -q p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        ",
  StyleBox["[Transitivity]",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  " Suppose ",
  StyleBox[" f - g == q p ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["g - h == r p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Then, ",
  StyleBox["f - g + g - h == f - h == q p + r p == (q + r) p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Equivalence modulo a non-zero polynomial preserves addition, \
subtraction, and multiplication of polynomials."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Proof",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Suppose that ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1 ",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  "is equivalent to ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is equivalent to ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        We want to show that ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["\[PlusMinus]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2 ",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  "is equivalent to ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["\[PlusMinus]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        And also that ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2 ",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  "is equivalent to ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Now ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" == q p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" == r p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " for suitable choices of ",
  StyleBox["q",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["r",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Therefore, ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" +",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" == f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["+",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["(f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" +",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[") == q p + r p == (q + r) p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Similarly, ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" -",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["(g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[") == f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["(f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" -",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[") == q p - r p == (q - r) p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Note that ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" == q g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" == r f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        So, ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" == q g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["- r f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["p == (q g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["1",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " ",
  StyleBox["- r f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[") p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Taking a fixed non-zero polynomial ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", the",
  StyleBox[" equivalence classes",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " modulo ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " can be added, subtracted, and multiplied (",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " representatives), and the algebra of equivalence classes satisfies all \
the familiar equations from ordinary algebra."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Proof",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        In technical terms, mathematicians say that this definition makes \
a ",
  StyleBox["homomorphic \n        image",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the algebra of polynomials which preserves all those usual equational \
laws. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Let us use the",
  StyleBox[" temporary notation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["Eq[f]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p ",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  "for the ",
  StyleBox["equivalence class",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " modulo ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        If ",
  StyleBox["F",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["G",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are two equivalence classes, we define ",
  StyleBox["E + F ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to mean ",
  StyleBox["Eq[f + g]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  " \n        where",
  StyleBox[" f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a member of ",
  StyleBox["F",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a member of ",
  StyleBox["G",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        The previous theorem shows that the result is ",
  StyleBox["independent of the choice",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the \n        representatives, and so the operation ",
  StyleBox["E + F",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " on equivalence classes is ",
  StyleBox["well defined",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        We can do the same to define ",
  StyleBox["E - F ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and ",
  StyleBox[" E F",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        In other words, the ",
  StyleBox["algebraic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["operations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are defined so that these formulae hold:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["        \t\tEq[f]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" + Eq[g]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" == Eq[f + g]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p\n          \t\t",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["Eq[f]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" - Eq[g]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" == Eq[f - g]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p\n          \t\t",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["Eq[f]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["   Eq[g]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" == Eq[f  g]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        So much for the ",
  StyleBox["algebraic operations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", what about the ",
  StyleBox["algebraic laws",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ?"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Let us try the ",
  StyleBox["Distributive Law",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as an example."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        In terms of ",
  StyleBox["equivalence classes",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", this reads:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["        \t\t(F + G) H == F H + G H",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        In terms of ",
  StyleBox["representatives",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", this reads equivalently:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["        \t\t(Eq[f]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" + Eq[g]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[") Eq[h]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p ",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["== Eq[f]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p ",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["Eq[h]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" + Eq[g]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" Eq[h]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Now, the ",
  StyleBox["LHS",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " can be written as:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["        \t\t(Eq[f]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" + Eq[g]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[") Eq[h]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p ",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["== Eq[(f + g) h]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        And, the ",
  StyleBox["RHS",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " can be written as:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["        \t\tEq[f]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p ",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["Eq[h]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" + Eq[g]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox[" Eq[h]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p ",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}],
  StyleBox["== Eq[f h + g h]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Subscript"}]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["        ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "But the equation ",
  StyleBox["(f + g) h == f h + g h",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is always true for ",
  StyleBox["polynomials",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ";\n        hence the analogous equation must be true for ",
  StyleBox["equivalence classes",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
        Any other equation valid for all polynomials can be treated \
in a similar fashion.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
        And so this is the way to transfer valid equations from \
polynomials over to equivalence classes.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Fortunately ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " has ",
  StyleBox["reduction",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " modulo a polynomial already built in \[LongDash] at least for polynomials \
with ",
  StyleBox["integer coefficients",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  So we can use the idea to compare the polynomial manipulation to the \
built-in complex number operations.  Fortunately, for the arithmetic \
operations, it is sufficient to manipulate polynomials with integer \
coefficients (but in several variables) for what we need to do.  We will see \
that the polynomial construction agrees exactly with the built-in complex \
operations.."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\((Collect[#1, i]&)\)[
      PolynomialMod[\((a + i\ b)\) + \((c + i\ d)\), i\^2 + 1]]\), 
    \(\((Collect[#1, i]&)\)[
      PolynomialMod[\((a + i\ b)\) - \((c + i\ d)\), i\^2 + 1]]\), 
    \(\((Collect[#1, i]&)\)[
      PolynomialMod[\((a + i\ b)\)\ \((c + i\ d)\), i\^2 + 1]]\), 
    \(\((Collect[#1, i]&)\)[PolynomialMod[\((a + i\ b)\)\^6, i\^2 + 1]]\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Therefore",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " \[LongDash] by using polynomial calculations \[LongDash] it is ",
  StyleBox["fully justified",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to use laws of algebra on complex numbers. ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Inverses and conjugates", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now polynomials themselves do not allow for ",
  StyleBox["division",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (there is a polynomial division with ",
  StyleBox["a remainder",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", however, but that is not what we mean).  An extra feature of the \
construction is needed to show that division is possible.  We earlier saw (",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["ComplexSimplify",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ") one reason why the complex numbers do have division.  We want also to \
discuss this feature in the light of the structure of polynomials (in ",
  StyleBox["i",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ") modulo the polynomial ",
  StyleBox["i",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  StyleBox[" + 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Observe that a certain product ",
  StyleBox["eliminates",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["i",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " from the arbitrary combination ",
  StyleBox["a + i b",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", as we can see this way:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PolynomialMod[\((a + i\ b)\)\ \((a - i\ b)\), i\^2 + 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell["This is why we could accept this definition of inverses:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ComplexSimplify[1\/\(a + I\ b\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Look at it this way.  Assuming that division is possible, we can think \
operationally of multiplying the top and bottom of the fraction ",
  StyleBox["1/(a + I b) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " by the complex number ",
  StyleBox["(a - I b)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " in order to get rid of the ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " in the denominator.  Then the result is in standard form with \
identifiable real and an imaginary parts.  We then take the resulting formula \
as the ",
  StyleBox["definition ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "of reciprocal.  But, subsequently, we have to check that the operation has \
the right properties. (We did this earlier.)  Note again that the discussion \
shows that the inverse exists for all complex numbers for which the quantity \
",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  StyleBox[" + b",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " equal to",
  StyleBox[" 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Since ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["b",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are real, this means that ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "of the two components is not ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Thus, as we remarked earlier, ",
  StyleBox["0 == 0 + I 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is the ",
  StyleBox["only",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " complex number ",
  StyleBox["without a multiplicative inverse",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This method just illustrated is one of using ",
  StyleBox["conjugates",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to clear denominators.  Conjugates also arise in another way from the \
algrbra.  If we take any polynomial ",
  StyleBox["f[i]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " in ",
  StyleBox["i",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and replace ",
  StyleBox["i",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " by ",
  StyleBox["\[Dash]i",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " we get another polynomial.  It should be clear that this substitution \
operation ",
  StyleBox["preserves sums and products",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  It also leaves the special polynomial ",
  StyleBox["i",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  StyleBox[" + 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["invariant",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".   It thus follows that the substitution ",
  StyleBox["preserves taking equivalence classes",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " modulo this polynomial.  So this operation is ",
  StyleBox["well defined",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on all complex numbers.  It is written in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as ",
  StyleBox["Conjugate[z].",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Conjugate[5 + I\ 7]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ComplexSimplify[Conjugate[a + I\ b]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Obviously,  if we do this substitution twice we get back to the original \
combination, whatever it was.  The conclusion is that this conjugation \
operation of interchanging ",
  StyleBox["i",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["\[Dash]i",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is an ",
  StyleBox["automorphism",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the algebraic structure of the complex numbers.  Note that the ",
  StyleBox["fixed points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of this transformation are exactly the real numbers."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Surprisingly, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " always cancels two uses of ",
  StyleBox["Conjugate ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "(as it does with a double ",
  StyleBox["Minus",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "):"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Conjugate[Conjugate[z]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-\ \(-\ z\)\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can check further the behavior and automorphism properties as follows.  \
In these operations, the command ",
  StyleBox["ComplexSimplify",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is called as ",
  StyleBox["ComplexSimplify[{z}] ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "or ",
  StyleBox["ComplexSimplify[{z, w}] ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "to indicate that the variables ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " should be treated as a ",
  StyleBox["complex",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " quantities, rather than as a ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " quantities\[LongDash]which is the usual convention with variables in \
expressions."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[\ ComplexSimplify[{z}], \n\t{a, Conjugate[a], z, Conjugate[z]}]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is treated as real and is left fixed under ",
  StyleBox["Conjugate",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\[LongDash]after simplification.  So, in this way we can check that \
addition, multiplication and division are preserved under conjgation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[ComplexSimplify[{z, w}], 
      Conjugate[z + w] == Conjugate[z] + Conjugate[w]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[ComplexSimplify[{z, w}], 
      Conjugate[z\ w] == Conjugate[z] Conjugate[w]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[ComplexSimplify[{z, w}], 
      Conjugate[z/w] == Conjugate[z]/Conjugate[w]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],
  StyleBox[" There are ",
    Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],
  StyleBox["no other",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic",
    Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],
  StyleBox[
  " non-trivial automorphisms of the ring of complex numbers aside from ",
    Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],
  StyleBox["Conjugate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],
  StyleBox[" leaving all the reals fixed.",
    Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Any such automorphism ",
  StyleBox["F",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " has to permute the roots of the equation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z\^2 + 1 == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?)  Since we have ",
  StyleBox["F[a] == a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " for all real ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", if ",
  StyleBox["F[I] == I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then for any ",
  StyleBox["z == a + I b",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we must have ",
  StyleBox["F[z] == z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  In this case, ",
  StyleBox["F",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is the trivial identity automorphism.  If, on the other hand,  ",
  StyleBox["F[I] == -I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then for any ",
  StyleBox["z == a + I b",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we must have ",
  StyleBox["F[z] == Conjugate[z]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  (Why?).  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Interesting Question.  ",
  StyleBox["Do the complex numbers have ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["any other",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" automorphisms (not keeping the reals fixed)?",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["\<\
Unfortunately, we do not have time in this course to work this out.\
\
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Geometric Representations", "Section",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Argand diagrams", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Definition and examples", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "An ",
  StyleBox["Argand diagram",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is a two-dimensional (plane) representation of a complex number ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", where\n                                   ",
  StyleBox["x = Re[z]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is plotted on the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis, and \n                                   ",
  StyleBox["y = Im[z]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is plotted on the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis. \nIn other words, we represent the complex number ",
  StyleBox["x + I y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " graphically in the same way we represent the 2-dimensional vector ",
  StyleBox["{x, y}",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " as a ",
  StyleBox["point in the plane",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with these coordinates.  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For example, consider again the two roots of the equation ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  StyleBox[" + 1 == 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  An Argand diagram of the roots is produced by the function ",
  StyleBox["RootPlot",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (defined in the notebook ",
  StyleBox["Implementations.nb",
    FontFamily->"Helvetica",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ")."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[z]; \)\), 
    \(\(RootPlot[z\^2 + 1 == 0, z]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Moreover, ",
  StyleBox["any collection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (",
  StyleBox["i.e.,",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " list) of complex numbers can be plotted using  ",
  StyleBox["ArgandPlot",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Here is a square array."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(tt = Flatten[Table[x + I\ y, {x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}]]; \n
    \(ArgandPlot[tt]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We can square and cube these numbers with very brief commands and \
plot the results.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ArgandPlot[tt\^2]; \)\), 
    \(ArgandPlot[tt\^3]; \nArgandPlot[tt\^4]; \)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We perhaps need an ",
  StyleBox["explanation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as to why these geometric patterns come out as they do."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Some roots of polynomials", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Another interesting equation is  ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["3",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  StyleBox[" + 1 == 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Here are its roots  (in two notations) and an Argand plot of its roots, \
and\[LongDash]after that\[LongDash]a plot for a related equation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Solve[z\^3 + 1 == 0, z]\), 
    \(Map[ComplexSimplify[{}], Solve[z\^3 + 1 == 0, z], 3]\), 
    \(\(RootPlot[z\^3 + 1 == 0, z]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Solve[z\^3 - 1 == 0, z]\), 
    \(Map[ComplexSimplify[{}], Solve[z\^3 - 1 == 0, z], 3]\), 
    \(\(RootPlot[z\^3 - 1 == 0, z]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The second set are called the  ",
  StyleBox["cube roots of unity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Note that in both cases the roots form a perfect ",
  StyleBox["equilateral",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["triangle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Algebraically, we can see where they come from better by factoring."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Factor[z\^3 + 1]\), 
    \(Factor[z\^3 - 1]\), 
    \(Factor[z\^6 - 1]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There are two obvious linear factors and two quadratic factors.  The cube \
roots of ",
  StyleBox["\[PlusMinus]1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " together form the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(6\^th\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["roots",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of unity. The roots of one quadratic factor are:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(RootPlot[1 - z + z\^2 == 0, z, 10, 
      PlotRange -> \ {{\(-1\), 1}, {\(-1\), 1}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now the roots for any quadratic equation can always be found by the well \
known ",
  StyleBox["Quadratic Formula ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "we learned at school."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[a\ z\^2 + b\ z + c\  == \ 0, z]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "A long as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(b\^2 - 4\ a\ c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["non negative",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the roots are ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", but in the other cases the roots are complex.  The ",
  StyleBox["cube roots",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of unity thus need only ",
  StyleBox["square roots",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in order to express them symbolically.  The fourth roots of unity are \
simpler:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[z\^4 - 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The fifth roots of unity ",
  StyleBox["might",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " present a more difficult problem.  Let us see."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[z\^5 - 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[ComplexSimplify[{}], \ \n\t
      Solve[1 + z + z\^2 + z\^3 + z\^4 == 0, z], \ 3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, to get the complex fifth roots of unity, some ",
  StyleBox["iterated square roots",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are needed, but the expressions are not too bad.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "By the way, this algebraic result means that the regular pentagon ",
  StyleBox["can",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " be constructed by ruler and compasses alone \[LongDash] as was already \
known to Euclid.  (Why?)"
}], "SmallText",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As easily seen by vector algebra, we could express ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " complex numbers in terms of ",
  StyleBox["1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the cube roots of unity.  This kind of a change of basis would show us \
some other interesting algebraic properties of the complex field.  But other \
equations also have roots, and let us try the ",
  StyleBox["7th roots of unity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(RootPlot[z\^7 - 1 == 0, z]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "What we see here in the Argand diagram is an example of the ",
  StyleBox["equal division",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the unit circle into ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " equal parts as the solutions of the equation ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["n",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  StyleBox[" - 1 == 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Again, we need an explanation as to why this nice geometric pattern is \
always obtained.  The explanation  will need some trigonometry, as we shall \
see later, for the regular heptagon ",
  StyleBox["cannot",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "be constructed by ruler and compasses, and the best we can do in notating \
the real and imaginary parts of the roots is as follows:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[ComplexSimplify[{}], \ \n\tSolve[z\^7 - 1 == 0, z], \ 3]\)], "Input"],

Cell["And let us try a different equation.  ", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(RootPlot[z\^12 + 1 == 0, z]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
This polynomial breakes up into two (real) factors, each with its \
own root plot.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ff = List@@Factor[z\^12 + 1]\n\), 
    \(\(\((RootPlot[# == 0, z]&)\)/@ff; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is a picture of the 7th roots of a complex number. ",
  "We see ",
  StyleBox["equal spacing",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " again seems to be found, but a ",
  StyleBox["rotational shift",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " has been included.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(RootPlot[z\^7 - \((20 + 35\ I)\) == 0, z]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Roots of other equations can be plotted as well.  For example:\
\>",
   "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(RootPlot[z\^7 + z\^5 - z\^4 + 1 == 0, z]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And this time the spacing is more complicated.  But note the roots are ",
  StyleBox["symmetric",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " about the real axis.  If an equation has ",
  StyleBox["complex coefficients",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", this may not be so."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(RootPlot[z\^7 + z\^5 - I\ z\^4 + 1 == 0, z]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
And here we see the roots of related polynomials as one coefficient \
is changed slightly.  (Be sure to animate the sequence of graphics!)\
\>", 
  "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Do[\(RootPlot[z\^7 + z\^5 - \((a + I\ a)\)\ z\^4 + 1 == 0, z, 10, \n\t
        PlotRange -> {{\(-2\), 2}, {\(-2\), 2}}]; \), {a, \(-4\), 4, 0.5}]
      \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Important Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  ",
  StyleBox[
  "Why can roots of other, higher-degree polynomial equations all be written \
in the form ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["a + I b",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" where ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" is the root of only a quadratic equation?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This fact has been ",
  StyleBox["illustrated",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in a few cases using ",
  StyleBox["RootPlot",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", but it has not been ",
  StyleBox["justified",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  In the other direction, here is a way to obtain a polynomial with all \
49 of these given complex numbers as roots.  (This trick could be applied to \
",
  StyleBox["any",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " table of complex numbers.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(tt = Flatten[Table[x + I\ y, {x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}]]; 
    pp[z_] := Expand[Times@@\((z - tt)\)]; \), 
    \(pp[z]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We can check this is correct by looking at the plot of the roots.\
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(RootPlot[pp[z] == 0, z]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that if we try to factor ",
  StyleBox["pp[z]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we get the ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " factors, where in this case the quadratic factors hide pairs of complex \
roots."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[pp[z]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To see all the roots, it is necessary to factor with ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " as an extension."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[pp[z], Extension \[Rule] {I}]\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Complex operations", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The arithmetic operations", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The first two theorems about the geometric meaning of ",
  StyleBox["addition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["subtraction",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of complex numbers need no proof, since the results are the direct \
translation of what we know about ",
  StyleBox["vector",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " addition and subtraction.  ",
  StyleBox["Multiplication",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", however,  requires a little knowledge of  ",
  StyleBox["trigonometry",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["Addition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of complex numbers is represented geometrically by the",
  StyleBox[" paralellogram  law",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for addition of vectors in the plane. "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = 2 + I\ 7; v = 4 + I\ 3; \), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[{0, u, v, u + v}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = 2 + I\ 7; v = 4 + I\ 3; \), 
    \(pg = {0, u, u + v, v, 0, u + v}; \n\n
    ap = ArgandPlot[{0, u, v, u + v}, 10, DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; 
    \n\nll = Show[Graphics[Line[\(({Re[#1], Im[#1]}&)\)/@pg]], 
        DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; \n\n
    Show[{ap, ll}, DisplayFunction \[Rule] $DisplayFunction]; \)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["Subtraction",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  is then represented as finding the ",
  StyleBox["other side of the parallelogram",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that makes the first given complex number the diagonal."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = 2 + I\ 7; v = 4 + I\ 3; \), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[{0, u, v, u - v}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = 2 + I\ 7; v = 4 + I\ 3; \), 
    \(pg = {0, u, v, 0, u - v, u}; \n\n
    ap = ArgandPlot[{0, u, v, u - v}, 10, DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; 
    \n\nll = Show[Graphics[Line[\(({Re[#1], Im[#1]}&)\)/@pg]], 
        DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; \n\n
    Show[{ap, ll}, DisplayFunction \[Rule] $DisplayFunction]; \)}], "Input"],

Cell["Now for multiplication and division.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["Multiplication",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of complex numbers is represented geometrically by the rule of ",
  StyleBox["adding the angles",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["multiplying the lengths",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the vectors."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["First, we draw some pictures.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = 1 + \(4\ I\)\/3; v = 3 + I\ 2; \), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[{0, 1, u, v, u\ v}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["This can be understood better if we draw in some triangles.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = 1 + \(4\ I\)\/3; v = 3 + I\ 2; \), 
    \(pg = {0, 1, v, 0, u, u\ v, 0}; \n\n
    ap = ArgandPlot[{0, 1, u, v, u\ v}, 10, 
        DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; \n\n
    ll = Show[Graphics[Line[\(({Re[#1], Im[#1]}&)\)/@pg]], 
        DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; \n\n
    Show[{ap, ll}, DisplayFunction \[Rule] $DisplayFunction]; \)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Explanation. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "One complex number ",
  StyleBox["v ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is joined to  ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " forming a triangle.  This triangle is rotated so the unit side is on the \
vector of the other complex number ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". The triangle is then magnified so the unit side is ",
  StyleBox["exactly",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the same as the other complex number.  The tip of the resulting triangle \
is then the ",
  StyleBox["product",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["u v ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "of the two complex numbers.  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is another picture with ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["v",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " interchanged."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(u = 3 + I\ 2; v = 1 + \(4\ I\)\/3; \npg = {0, 1, v, 0, u, u\ v, 0}; \n\n
    ap = ArgandPlot[{0, 1, u, v, u\ v}, 10, 
        DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; \n\n
    ll = Show[Graphics[Line[\(({Re[#1], Im[#1]}&)\)/@pg]], 
        DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; \n\n
    Show[{ap, ll}, DisplayFunction \[Rule] $DisplayFunction]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And here is a picture of ",
  StyleBox["iterated",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " multiplications."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(z = 3 + I\ 1; w = 4\/5 + \(I\ 3\)\/5; \), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[{0, 1, w, z}, 8]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[Join[{0, 1, w}, Table[z\ w\^n, {n, 0, 8}]], 8]; \)\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We now give the ",
  StyleBox["trigonometric proof",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the multiplication theorem."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Consider a complex number ",
  StyleBox["z == a + b \[ImaginaryI]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Rewrite it as:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        StyleBox[
          \(\@\(a\^2 + b\^2\)\ 
            \((a\/\@\(a\^2 + b\^2\) + \(\[ImaginaryI]\ b\)\/\@\(a\^2 + b\^2\))
              \)\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], TraditionalForm]]]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here ",
  StyleBox["r == ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        StyleBox[\(\@\(a\^2 + b\^2\)\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], TraditionalForm]]],
  " is a non-negative ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " number (the ",
  StyleBox["length",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the vector, also called ",
  StyleBox["\[LeftBracketingBar]z\[RightBracketingBar]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ").  Now ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        StyleBox[\(\ 
          \((a\/\@\(a\^2 + b\^2\) + \(\[ImaginaryI]\ b\)\/\@\(a\^2 + b\^2\))
            \)\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], TraditionalForm]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is a ",
  StyleBox["unit vector",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with its tip on the ",
  StyleBox["unit circle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This vector makes a certain angle ",
  StyleBox["\[Theta]",
    FontFamily->"Symbol",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  with the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis (the ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["axis",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "), which is called ",
  StyleBox["Arg(z)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the ",
  StyleBox["very definition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["Sin",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["Cos",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " we can write:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "                             ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        RowBox[{\(a\/\@\(a\^2 + b\^2\)\), 
          StyleBox["==",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], \(cos(\[Theta])\)}], TraditionalForm]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`b\/\@\(a\^2 + b\^2\) == sin(\[Theta])\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So we have",
  StyleBox[" z == r (cos(\[Theta]) + \[ImaginaryI] sin(\[Theta]))",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  This is called the ",
  StyleBox["polar form",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the complex number ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Suppose  ",
  StyleBox["w == s (cos(\[Phi])+\[ImaginaryI] sin(\[Phi]))",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is another complex number.  Then we find:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox[
"\t z w == r s (cos(\[Theta]) cos(\[Phi]) - sin(\[Theta]) sin(\[Phi]) + \
\[ImaginaryI] (cos(\[Phi]) sin(\[Theta]) + cos(\[Theta]) sin(\[Phi]))).",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Buy the ",
  StyleBox["Trigonometric",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["Addition Laws",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " this can be rewritten as:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "\t ",
  StyleBox[
  "z w == r s (cos(\[Theta]+\[Phi])+\[ImaginaryI] sin(\[Theta]+\[Phi]))",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is the",
  StyleBox[" polar form",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the vector required by the statement of the theorem.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We should recap what we learned from this proof about the ",
  StyleBox["polar form",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of complexes."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Every complex number ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " can be written ",
  StyleBox["uniquely",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in the (traditional) form\n                                   ",
  StyleBox[
  " z == \[LeftBracketingBar]z\[RightBracketingBar] \
(cos(Arg(z))+\[ImaginaryI] sin(Arg(z)))",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ",\nwhere ",
  StyleBox[
  "\[LeftBracketingBar]z\[RightBracketingBar] == Abs(z) \[GreaterEqual] 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["-\[Pi] < Arg(z) \[LessEqual] \[Pi].",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " actually has this law built in ",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a special use of ",
  StyleBox["ComplexExpand",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (but we will not often use that command this way):"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[z]\), 
    \(ComplexExpand[z, {z}, TargetFunctions \[Rule] {Arg, Abs}] // Factor
      \)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For example, as regards ",
  StyleBox["Arg",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " we have the following results, clearly showing the limits on the values \
of the function:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Arg/@{1, I, \(-I\), \(-1\), \(-1\) - 0.00001\ I}\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The function is discontinuus along the negative real axis, as is \
shown in the next plot.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Plot3D[Arg[a + I\ b], {a, \(-1\), 1}, {b, \(-1\), 1}, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 31]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Nevertheless, it is a very useful function.  We turn next to \
division.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["Division",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of complex numbers is represented geometrically by the rule of ",
  StyleBox["subtracting the angles",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["dividing the lengths",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the vectors."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "No proof is really required here because ",
  StyleBox[" u == z/w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is equivalent to ",
  StyleBox["u w == z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Here are some pictures."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = 1 + I\ 5; v = 4 + I\ 3; \), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[{0, 1, u, v, u\/v}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(z = 2 + I\ 1; w = 4\/3 + I; Abs[w]\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[{0, 1, w, z, z\/w}]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[Table[z\/w\^n, {n, 0, 9}]]; \)\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Powers and conjugates", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As a corollary to the multiplication and division theorems, we have (",
  StyleBox["by induction",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ") this important result for powers of complex numbers: "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "For every complex number ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and every integer ",
  StyleBox["n ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "(",
  StyleBox["including",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " negative integers),\n                   ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(z\^n == Abs[z]\^n\ \((Cos[n\ Arg[z]] + I\ Sin[n\ Arg[z]])\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we should note that as a consequence of this formula, the powers of a \
complex number are always ",
  StyleBox["equally spaced",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as far as ",
  StyleBox["angles",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are concerned.  The ",
  StyleBox["lengths",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (",
  StyleBox["==",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " distances from the origin) may well change, of course, since  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Abs[z\^n] == Abs[z]\^n\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["Conjugation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of complex numbers is represented geometrically by ",
  StyleBox["reflection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " about the real axis."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["A picture shows us why this is so.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ArgandPlot[{0, \n\t\t2 + I\ 7, Conjugate[2 + I\ 7], \n\t\t6 - I\ 11, 
        Conjugate[6 - I\ 11], 14}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Some applications of all these operations are illustrated in the \
optional sections below.\
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Rotations and Spirals (Optional)", "Section",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
  "Rotations ",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " complex numbers (optional)"
}], "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Rotating a grid", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is frequently convenient to reduce a complex number to a unit vector, \
and we can do this by a ",
  StyleBox["normalizarion operation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This is convenient for determining rotations."
}], "Text"],

Cell["??Norm", "Input"],

Cell["Let us make a portion of a square grid of points.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(tt = Flatten[Table[x + I\ y, {x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}]]; \)\), 
    \(\(aa = ArgandPlot[tt]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We recall that multiplication by a complex number is a ",
  StyleBox["rotation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and possibly a ",
  StyleBox["change of scale",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  However, in case the complex number is ",
  StyleBox["normalized",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (",
  StyleBox["i.e.,",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "has length ",
  StyleBox["1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and, thus, represents a unit vector), then multiplication by this number \
is simply a rotation.   Here is a pure change of scale (",
  StyleBox["i.e., ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "multiplication by a ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "number)."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(bb = ArgandPlot[2\ tt]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
It looks the same as before, of course.  But here is the \
comparison.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Show[{aa, bb}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now let us illustrate a rotation through ",
  StyleBox["45",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["o",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(z = Norm[1 + I]\), 
    \(\(Show[{ArgandPlot[{0, z, 1 + I}], Graphics[Circle[{0, 0}, 1]]}]; 
    \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[N[z]\ tt]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next, to rotate through ",
  StyleBox["60",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["o",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  ", we have to solve a cube-root equation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(u =. ; \)\), 
    \(\(Show[{RootPlot[u\^3 + 1 == 0, u], Graphics[Circle[{0, 0}, 1]]}]; 
    \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(w = 
      ComplexExpand[
        u /. \(Solve[u\^3 + 1 == 0]\)\[LeftDoubleBracket]3
            \[RightDoubleBracket]]\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[N[w]\ tt]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Finally, let us use this number ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to generate a grid, and then rotate it through ",
  StyleBox["60",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["o",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  " as well as through ",
  StyleBox["45",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["o",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(uu = Flatten[Table[N[i + w\ j], {i, \(-5\), 5}, {j, \(-5\), 5}]]; 
    \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[uu]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[N[w]\ uu]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[N[z]\ uu]; \)\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Geometric transformations", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Inasmuch as addition of complex numbers is geometrically ",
  StyleBox["addition of vectors",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then a very simple transformation such as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z \[Rule] a + b\ z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", where ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a given, fixed complex number, represents a ",
  StyleBox["parallel",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["displacement",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "or ",
  StyleBox["translation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  in the direction of ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".   When we combine these with ",
  StyleBox["multiplication",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by complex numbers, we get a very useful class of geometric \
transformations."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  All ",
  StyleBox["orientation preserving rigid motions",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the Euclidean plane can be written as a transformation of complex \
numbers of the form\n                                                    ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z \[Rule] a + b\ z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",",
  StyleBox["\n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "where ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " represents the ",
  StyleBox["displacement",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (translation) of the origin and the ",
  StyleBox["non-zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " complex number ",
  StyleBox["b == Norm[b] ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "represents a ",
  StyleBox["rotation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " through angle ",
  StyleBox["Arg[b]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".   \nIf we do not require ",
  StyleBox["b",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to be normalized, then the transformation also includes a ",
  StyleBox["magnification",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by factor ",
  StyleBox["Abs[b]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  (Those are called",
  StyleBox[" similarity transformations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".)  \nMoreover, all ",
  StyleBox["orientation reversing motions",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the Euclidean plane can be written as a transformation of complex \
numbers of the form\n                                              ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z \[Rule] a + b\ Conjugate[z]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We do not have time to develop the background here to prove such a theorem \
(",
  StyleBox["e.g.",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " defining ",
  StyleBox["rigid motions",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["similarity transformations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "), but we can illustrate how the simple algebraic definition of such \
transformations can be used to solve problems."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The ",
  StyleBox["composition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of non-identity rotations about distinct centers is either a ",
  StyleBox["rotation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " about a third center or is a ",
  StyleBox["translation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Proof:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Suppose we want to ",
  StyleBox["rotate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the plane about a point ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " as the ",
  StyleBox["center",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the rotation,\n        using a ",
  StyleBox["unit vector",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to determine the ",
  StyleBox["angle of rotation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Then, starting with any point ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we move back to the origin ",
  StyleBox["opposite",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        This takes us to ",
  StyleBox["z - p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Then we ",
  StyleBox["rotate around the origin",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " getting to ",
  StyleBox["u (z - p)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Finally, we move ",
  StyleBox["foward",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", getting to ",
  StyleBox["p + u (z-p)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        This is what we want, because this transformation (of ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ") has ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " as a ",
  StyleBox["fixed point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", \n        and it takes any vector ",
  StyleBox["p + w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to the vector ",
  StyleBox["p + u w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Note that if ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is the number ",
  StyleBox["1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " then the transformation is the ",
  StyleBox["identity transformation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Now suppose we have ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " such transformations, and we assume ",
  StyleBox["neither",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is the identity."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[p, q, u, v, z, f, g]; \)\), 
    \(\(f[z_] := p + u\ \((z - p)\); \)\), 
    \(\(g[z_] := q + v\ \((z - q)\); \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        We want to take the ",
  StyleBox["composition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the two rotations."
}], "Text"],

Cell["f[g[z]] // Expand", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["        ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "There are two cases: either ",
  StyleBox["u v == 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " or ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        In the first case the transformation has the form ",
  StyleBox["(p - q)(1 - u) + z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ",\n        and it represents a ",
  StyleBox["translation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (of an amount we can calculate, as shown)."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        In the second case, we can solve for the ",
  StyleBox["fixed-point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (or ",
  StyleBox["center",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ") of the transformation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Simplify[Solve[f[g[z]] == z, {z}]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        We can name this point ",
  StyleBox["r",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["r = z /. %[[1]]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Then we check that the composition is indeed a ",
  StyleBox["rotation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (by ",
  StyleBox["u v",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ") about ",
  StyleBox["r",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Simplify[f[g[z]] == r + u\ v\ \((z - r)\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        Note we can write ",
  StyleBox["r",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " in a different form."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(w = \(1 - u\)\/\(1 - u\ v\); \)\), 
    \(Simplify[r == p\ w + q\ \((1 - w)\)]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "        That calculation shows ",
  StyleBox["r",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["not equal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to either ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " or ",
  StyleBox["q",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", because ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is neither ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " nor",
  StyleBox[" 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["        Q.E.D.",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Doing some iterations for fun (optional)", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Exponential spirals", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
Let us take successive powers of a complex number and look at their \
plot.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(u = 1.08\ Norm[2 + I]; \)\), 
    \(\(f[z_] := u\ z; \)\), 
    \(\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 10]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see the beginning of a ",
  StyleBox["spiral",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and further iteration gives several turns of the spiral."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 60]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 5]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This kind of graph is a discrete version of an ",
  StyleBox["exponential spiral",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The continuous version can be drawn by using parametric plotting."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ParametricPlot[1.3\^t\ {Cos[t], Sin[t]}, {t, 0, 6\ \[Pi]}, 
      AspectRatio \[Rule] Automatic, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-150\), 150}, {\(-150\), 150}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["Without the exponential part, you just get the circle.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ParametricPlot[{Cos[t], Sin[t]}, {t, 0, 2\ \[Pi]}, 
      AspectRatio \[Rule] Automatic]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Here are some spirals where the opening up is not exponential\
\>", 
  "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ParametricPlot[t\ {Cos[t], Sin[t]}, {t, 0, 4\ \[Pi]}, 
      AspectRatio \[Rule] Automatic]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ParametricPlot[t\^2\ {Cos[t], Sin[t]}, {t, 0, 6\ \[Pi]}, 
      AspectRatio \[Rule] Automatic, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-300\), 400}, {\(-400\), 250}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["And for something entirely different:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ParametricPlot[{Cos[3\ t], Sin[4\ t]}, {t, 0, 2\ \[Pi]}, 
      AspectRatio \[Rule] Automatic]; \)\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The spiral of Theodorus", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is a discrete spiral that  was apparently known in ancient times.  \
The operation ",
  StyleBox["z ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "|",
  StyleBox["--> z + I Norm[z] ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "just adds a ",
  StyleBox["perpedicular unit vector",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the given vector (complex number) ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := N[z + I\ Norm[z]]; \)\), 
    \(\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 30]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["The lengths of the successive vectors are as follows:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Abs/@sp\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the ",
  StyleBox["Pythagorian Theorem",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", these numbers are just the square roots of the integers."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[Sqrt/@Range[20]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here is an extended plot of the spiral.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 500]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 5]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
It is not so easy to find the continuous analogue of this sprial, \
by the way.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
In making such a sprial, there is no reason the unit vector has to \
be taken as perpendicular.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = Norm[1 + 7\ I]\), 
    \(\(f[z_] := N[z + u\ Norm[z]]; \)\), 
    \(\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 20]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Here is an extended picture.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 300]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 5]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["With other angles, it is easy to run off the screen.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = Norm[11 - 7\ I]\), 
    \(\(f[z_] := N[z + u\ Norm[z]]; \)\), 
    \(\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 30]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 500]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 3]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Does this curve loop around or not?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A double whammy", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "You get some crazy pictures if you rotate ",
  StyleBox["both",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "the vector ",
  StyleBox["and",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the unit vector."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = Norm[30 - I]\), 
    \(v = I\n\), 
    \(f[z_] := N[u\ z + v\ Norm[z]]; \n\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 100]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
That is not so crazy, but further iteration shows there is \
apparently a limit to the lengths of the vectors.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 1000]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 5]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Here is what seems likely to be a related picture.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = Norm[1 - 30\ I]\), 
    \(v = I\n\), 
    \(f[z_] := N[u\ z + v\ Norm[z]]; \n\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 500]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 5]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["But the picture is rather different!", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 1000]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 5]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
It is easy to waste time (and computing power) on such trials.  \
Here is another:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = Norm[1 - 30\ I]\), 
    \(v = \(-I\)\), 
    \(ClearAll[f]; \nf[z_] := N[u\ z + v\ Norm[z]]; \n
    \(sp = NestList[f, 1, 100]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 5]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 500]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 5]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["And another:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = Norm[2 - 3\ I]\), 
    \(v = Norm[3 - I]\n\), 
    \(f[z_] := N[u\ z + v\ Norm[z]]; \n\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 200]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 5]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["And another:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = Norm[10 + 7\ I]\), 
    \(v = Norm[10 - 7\ I]\n\), 
    \(f[z_] := N[u\ z + v\ Norm[z]]; \n\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 200]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 5]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["And another:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = Norm[1 + I]\), 
    \(v = Norm[1 - I]\n\), 
    \(f[z_] := N[u\ z + v\ Norm[z]]; \n\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 500]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 5]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["And another:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = Norm[1 + 10\ I]\), 
    \(v = Norm[1 - 10\ I]\n\), 
    \(f[z_] := N[u\ z + v\ Norm[z]]; \n\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 200]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 5]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["And another:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(u = Norm[1 + 1000\ I]\), 
    \(v = u\^2\n\), 
    \(f[z_] := N[u\ z + v\ Norm[z]]; \n\(sp = NestList[f, 1, 300]; \)\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[sp, 5]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Enough!!!!!",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]]], "Text",
  FontSize->18]
}, Closed]]
